47:Against the deployed Worker, run `npm run access:env` once, then `npm run smoke -- https://stallion.kattebak.fyi/b/<id>` or `npm run demo:duo -- https://stallion.kattebak.fyi/`. Both scripts read `CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_ID` and `CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_SECRET` from the environment, else from `~/.config/stallion/access-env`, and send them as `CF-Access-Client-Id` and `CF-Access-Client-Secret` on every request of every browser context. The `smoke` job in `deploy.yml` runs the same smoke on `/b/ci-<run id>` once the live Worker serves the pushed commit, reading both values from the `CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_ID` and `CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_SECRET` repo secrets; a failing smoke fails the workflow.
73:Apply is local. `npm run infra:plan` and `npm run infra:apply` run `tofu init` and the command in `infra/`, reading `CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN` from `~/.config/stallion/cf-env`. The state lives outside the repo in `~/.config/stallion/terraform.tfstate` (directory 700, file 600); both scripts pass it to `tofu init -reconfigure` as `-backend-config=[REDACTED] and `infra/.gitignore` keeps any `*.tfstate` out of git. The state holds the service token client secret in plain text; never commit, delete or hand-edit it.
111:- A tile doc holds one root, the `Y.Map` `objects`, keyed by `objectId`. Each value is the `encode()` CBOR of the object as a `Uint8Array`. Strokes, shapes and text carry `colour`, the nearest palette index 0..5, and `rgb`, an integer 0..0xFFFFFF; objects and live ink written before `rgb` existed lack it, and readers derive it from `PALETTE_RGB[colour]`. Every object also carries `size`, the nearest of `Small`, `Medium` and `Large`, and `width`, a finite stroke width of 0.5 to 96 screen px at the object's native level; objects written before `width` lack it, and readers derive it from `PENCIL_PX[size]` (3, 8 and 20). A stroke also carries `style`: `Pen`, `Highlighter` (0.4 alpha, `multiply` compositing), `Dashed` (the centreline stroked with a dash, in place of the pressure outline) or `Uniform` (pressure fixed at 0.5); strokes and live ink written before `style` lack it and read as `Pen`. A shape carries `style` too, plus `kind` (`Rectangle`, `Ellipse`, `Line` or `Arrow`), tile-local `start` and `end` points (`[x, y]`; a rectangle or ellipse spans them as opposite corners, a line or arrow runs from `start` to `end`, and an arrow head grows with `width`), and `fill`: `None` or `Tint`, which fills a rectangle or ellipse at 0.2 alpha of `rgb`. A shape follows the same smallest-containing-tile placement as a stroke. `packages/schema/fixtures/shape.cbor.hex` pins a shape for both sides. A `Sticky` is its own type with no `colour`, `size` or `style`: `rgb` is the ink, `background` the note colour (0..0xFFFFFF), `width` the font size (0.5 to 96 px at the native level, scaled with the frame on resize), `bbox` the world-space note frame, and `text` at most 4096 UTF-8 bytes, which the server checks; the empty string is a new note. The client wraps `text` to the frame width with `wrapText` in `apps/web/src/sticky.ts` and grows the frame down to fit on commit. `packages/schema/fixtures/sticky.cbor.hex` pins a sticky for both sides. The codec always writes `rgb`, `width` and, on a stroke or shape, `style`. `packages/schema/fixtures/stroke-legacy.cbor.hex` pins a stroke with none of the three for both sides. The server validates every changed value with serde and rejects the whole update if one fails.
116:- A text frame or undecodable CBOR closes the socket with 1003 or 1007.
140:| `GET /api/me/ws` | | WebSocket upgrade (426 without one), hibernation API. The server pushes one binary CBOR map `{kind: "boards", rows, thumbnails}` on connect and after every accepted `PUT` or `DELETE`, to every open socket of that identity; `rows` is what `GET /api/me/boards` answers. When the frame would exceed 256 KB the rows go with empty thumbnails and `thumbnails: false`, and the client fetches the list over REST. The client sends nothing; a text frame closes it with 1003. |

